 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others to stay contiguous
+- Since: 2026-10-23 (maintainer override of the 2026-09-02 decision)
+- Decision: closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs `t1..tn` in order and resets `next` to `n+1`, so keys stay
+  contiguous (`t1,t2` after closing `t2` of `t1,t2,t3`).
+- Overridden by: the maintainer, explicitly referencing this record on 2026-10-23. No new measurement or defect was
+  cited; the earlier reason below was not refuted.
+- Known cost (accepted): the key is the React key and the URL state, so shifting keys remounts every tab to the right
+  of the closed one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29).
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys to stay contiguous", "open after close continues the
+  contiguous sequence")
+
+### §stable-tab-keys (original) — SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23
 - Since: 2026-09-02
 - Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
 - Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
 - Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the old "close keeps keys"
+  test).
+- Superseded by: §stable-tab-keys above.
